.TH AVR\-SERVICE\-TOKEN\-SET\-ACCESS 1 "" "avr" "Avrea CLI"
.SH NAME
avr-service-token-set-access \- Replace a service token's whole access selection.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avr service-token set-access
[OPTIONS] TOKEN_ID
.SH DESCRIPTION
Replace a service token's whole access selection.
.PP
The grants given here replace everything the token reaches: a grant you leave out is removed, so repeat the ones to keep. The command reads the token first and the change fails if the token changes in between. A grant that widens its access must be within your own access now. The API may ask for a recent sign-in; the command then exits 4 and says to run `avr auth login` again. The change applies to the token's next request.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Examples:
    avr service-token set-access <token-id> --repo acme/api --repo acme/web
    avr service-token set-access <token-id> --all-firewall-rules
.fi
.RE
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fITOKEN_ID\fR
\&
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR, \fB\-R\fR \fITEXT\fR
Grant read access to a repository (org/repo or rep-xxx). Repeatable. (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-firewall\-rule\fR \fITEXT\fR
Grant read access to an egress firewall rule, by rule ID (efr-xxx). Repeatable. (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-all\-firewall\-rules\fR
Grant read access to every egress firewall rule, including rules added later.
.TP
\fB\-\-org\fR, \fB\-o\fR \fITEXT\fR
Organization ID or slug. Uses default org if not specified (see: avr config set org).
